From 7fb47d5e17f7d8241d8616ef3f0bec0ea1f37707 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christian F.K. Schaller" <christian.schaller@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:32:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] scanner: Add GetFrameParameters, StartFrame and Cancel

This commit adds the frames: StartFrame returns the read end of a pipe
that carries the frame's pixels, FrameEvent reports the frame's progress,
Cancel stops it, and GetFrameParameters gives the layout the next frame
would have.

The portal creates the pipe and passes the write end to the helper over
the socket as a file descriptor, so the pixels do not pass through the
portal. Its own copy is closed at once, so that the application sees the
end of the data when the helper closes its.

The helper scans a frame in a thread of its own and goes on reading
requests, a Cancel above all. Only one thread talks to libsane at a
time, so while a frame is in progress the requests that need libsane
(GetOptions, GetOptionValues, SetOptions, GetFrameParameters and another
StartFrame) are refused as Busy. This is new for GetOptions and
GetOptionValues, and both draft interface files now say so. A frame
that is not the last of its scan is continued with sane_start and no
sane_cancel in between, since a real driver starts the scan over after a
cancel. The pipe is not blocking, so that an application that is not
reading cannot hold the thread past a Cancel. The application closing
its end of the pipe cancels the frame.

Events are sent by the helper as messages with serial 0 and read, with
the replies, by a reader thread per session that hands each reply to
the call waiting for it and turns each event into a FrameEvent signal
for the application that owns the session. Every event carries the
number of its frame. The frame's slot is freed before its last event is
sent, so that an application can start the next frame from its handler.
Messages can now carry a file descriptor, with SCM_RIGHTS.

The frame formats ir, rgbi and grayi, which sane.h keeps out of its
enumeration, are reported by name and the draft now lists them.

Writing to a pipe whose reader has gone raises SIGPIPE, and ignoring it
for the process was not enough: the test backend sets it back to the
default, which killed the helper. The frame's thread blocks it while it
writes and takes a pending one off before it unblocks.

Tested with SANE's test backend, on the helper alone over a socketpair
and through the portal on a private bus (20 checks): a whole frame with
its events and byte count, frame numbers, Busy during a frame, Cancel
and an early close of the pipe ending the frame, another application
refused, the helper killed in the middle of a frame (the pipe ends and
the application gets Session.Closed), the scanner opened again, and the
application leaving in the middle of a frame. Not tested: the call
timeout, a helper that stops answering without dying, two simultaneous
opens, a scanner with more than one frame per scan, and the 8200i.
